<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-3-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Reduction of Order </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Use reduction of order techniques to find additional solutions of ODEs. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <p>
        Consider a 2nd-order linear ODE. If we know one solution <m>y_1</m>, we can use it to obtain a second solution <m>y_2</m> that is linearly-indpendent using a process called <alert> reduction of order </alert>. 
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given that <m>y_1(x) = e^x </m> solves the problem <m>y'' - y = 0</m> on <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>, find another solution. 
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We assume that another solution likely has a similar form to <m>y_1</m>. But it may be that <m>y_2 := u(x)e^x </m>. This would mean
                <md>
                    <mrow> y_2' \amp = \deriv{}{x} [ u(x) e^x] = u'(x) e^x + u(x)e^x </mrow>
                    <mrow> \implies y_2'' \amp \deriv{}{x}[y_2'] = u''(x) e^x + 2u'(x) e^x + u(x)e^x </mrow>
                </md>
                Assume it has this form and is a solution to the problem on <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m>. Then we have
                <md>
                    y'' - y = u''(x) e^x + 2u'(x) e^x = (u'' + 2u')e^x = 0. 
                </md>
                There are no <m>x</m> values that can make <m>e^x = 0</m>, so it must be that <md> u'' + 2u' = 0.</md> In this way, we have created a new differential equation to help us find <m>u</m>. We can <alert> reduce the order </alert> of this equation by writing <m>v(x) = u'(x)</m>. The equation then becomes <md> v' + 2v = 0 </md>. This linear first-order equation has the solution <md> v(x) = e^{2x} \implies u(x) = \int e^{2x}\, dx \implies u(x) = \frac{1}{2}e^{2x}. </md> Therefore, the second solution to the original differential equation is <md> y_2(x) = \left(\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}\right) \cdot e^x = \frac{1}{2} e^{3x}. </md>
                
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Note:</alert> We started from a particular solution and obtained another particular solution. We do not need to track constants of integration in this case. 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

</section>
